es nasty remarks about colleagues. So stress resilience good, reliability good, but interpersonal issues (nasty remarks) affect crew cooperation, also memory for numbers could affect attention.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education. So decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence good, but communication and adaptability poor.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. So reliability, problem solving, responsibility good, but temperament and cooperation issues.\n\nWe need best overall fit. All have some concerns. Which candidate has strongest overall? Candidate B has stress resilience, conscientiousness, but interpersonal issue nasty remarks is serious for crew cooperation. Candidate C has calmness, decision quality, technical competence, but communication difficulty and egocentric. Candidate D has responsibility, concentration, problem solving, but hot temper and know-it-all may affect crew. Candidate A has leadership, anticipation, but show-off and not open to new ideas.\n\nWhich concerns are more severe? Nasty remarks (B) could be severe for crew. Hot temper (D) also severe. Egocentric and communication difficulty (C) also severe. Show-off and not open to new ideas (A) maybe less severe? Leadership good. Might be best overall? But need to consider stress resilience: no explicit for A. B handles stress well. C keeps calm in crisis. D responds adequately. So B, C have stress resilience.\n\nTechnical competence: A sees complex connections, spatial vision; C understands complicated technology; D solves problems extremely well; B has excellent computer skills.\n\nDecision quality: C can make correct decisions quickly. D solves problems extremely well. A anticipates dangerous situations.\n\nOverall, maybe Candidate C has strong decision, calmness, technical, but communication issues could be mitigated? However egocentric and unwilling to further education may affect adaptability.\n\nCandidate B has conscientiousness, stress handling, but nasty remarks could be a red flag for crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate D has responsibility, concentration, problem solving, but hot temper is serious.\n\nCandidate A has leadership, anticipation, but show-off and not open to new ideas may affect crew.\n\nWe need pick best overall. I think Candidate C's strengths align well with core pilot criteria: decision quality, calmness, technical competence. Communication difficulty is concerning but maybe can be trained. Egocentric and unwilling to further education are concerns for adaptability. But maybe less severe than nasty remarks or hot temper.\n\nThus I might recommend Candidate C.\n\nLet's produce message: state recommendation for Candidate C, reference strengths (quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, understands complicated technology) and concerns (communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education) and argue why strengths outweigh concerns compared to others.\n\nProceed."}
